## INV-nopoh: a Go call under a package-level `var` now emits a `calls` edge anchored on the variable

**Root cause.** `_get_enclosing_function` walked up to a `function_declaration`/`method_declaration` and returned `None` at the file root; the call arm skipped every call with no anchor. Every cobra `Run:` handler, every `var logger = log.New(...)`, every literal bound to a package variable emitted nothing. Measured on beads: 578 of 1,331 `fmt.Fprintf` sites (43.4%).

**Fix.** A call under a package-level `var_spec` anchors on that variable's own symbol (kind `variable`, already emitted); a blank `var _ = register()` anchors on the file pseudo-symbol; a literal inside a named function keeps its anchor. `_get_enclosing_func_name` (the `var_types` scope) returns the same name through one shared helper, so the WI-suhug writer classifier works inside a package-level body.

**Two more defects found by reading the new rows back, fixed in the same change.**
1. Grouped `var ( a = ...; b = ... )` blocks had NO variable symbols (the symbol pass iterated `var_declaration`'s direct children and never saw `var_spec_list`) and no interface-assertion detection. Both descend now.
2. The new alias symbols (`var ( NewWebhook = testutils.NewWebhook )`, two packages) made a dot-imported bare `NewWebhook()` a three-candidate ambiguity; the resolver's guard withheld it and 114 alertmanager sites fell from a resolved function to a placeholder in the first arm B. The dot-imported in-module paths are now offered as the resolver's path hint; the call binds to the dot-imported package's alias variable.

